ZAICODE

CONTINUATION AUTHORITY

Continue the existing T-1 implementation from the current archive state.

Do not create a replacement ticket for the same user request unless the project protocol requires a child ticket for independently tracked work.

The current archive is still at the early BUILD stage:
- upstream ZCode baseline has been inspected;
- isolated bootstrap has been started and demonstrated externally;
- the repository remains largely upstream;
- root UI.md does not yet exist;
- ZAICODE product identity is not yet implemented;
- account independence is not yet implemented;
- ZAICODE agent-management and queue surfaces are not yet implemented.

This handoff extends the existing T-1 acceptance target.

It supersedes narrower continuation instructions for T-1 but preserves all valid requirements from SRC-001.

AUTONOMOUS EXECUTION

Continue sequentially through eligible milestones without asking the operator to continue.

After every bounded implementation slice:
1. verify focused behavior;
2. inspect regressions around the changed boundary;
3. update project state/evidence according to the repository protocol;
4. select the next eligible milestone automatically.

Before context exhaustion:
- checkpoint exact state;
- record completed verification;
- record unresolved risks;
- record the next exact action;
- preserve enough evidence for cold recovery.

Stop only for a real external blocker.

PRIMARY PRODUCT DECISION

ZAICODE is a standalone local application derived from the ZCode source tree.

ZCode provides the editor/workspace/chat/runtime/UI foundation.

ZAICODE must not require a Z.ai application account.

The intended long-term execution path is:

ZAICODE UI
→ ZAICODE Agent / Work Queue
→ SAIFREN
→ SAIRoute
→ 9router
→ selected provider/model
→ ZCode-derived execution runtime
→ result/state back to ZAICODE UI

Do not implement the full external SAIFREN/SAIRoute/9router integration speculatively if their concrete contracts are not yet available.

Instead establish clean extension boundaries that allow those systems to become the routing backend without another UI/runtime rewrite.

MILESTONE A — VERIFY BASELINE RECEIPT

Before product changes, complete and preserve the upstream baseline receipt.

Record:
- official upstream URL;
- branch;
- exact commit SHA;
- ZCode source version;
- Apache-2.0 license obligations;
- Node pin from mise.toml;
- pnpm pin from mise.toml;
- actual bootstrap result;
- CLI smoke result;
- typecheck result;
- lint result;
- Desktop smoke result.

The repository pins:
- Node 24.14.0;
- pnpm 10.33.2.

Do not upgrade pnpm merely because a newer version is advertised.

Do not mutate the user's unrelated global Node/pnpm installation when the pinned project-local toolchain can be used.

Classify existing upstream lint warnings separately from ZAICODE regressions.

MILESTONE B — PRODUCT IDENTITY SEPARATION

Create a real ZAICODE desktop identity.

The current upstream product identity model contains production and preview ZCode identities.

Extend this architecture rather than performing scattered string replacement.

Create one authoritative ZAICODE product flavor or equivalent centralized identity configuration.

ZAICODE must have its own, non-colliding values for applicable identity fields:

- product name;
- Electron appId;
- Windows AppUserModelId;
- executable/package identity;
- userData root;
- local application data;
- logs;
- temporary runtime data;
- single-instance lock namespace;
- deep-link scheme if enabled;
- updater identity/channel if enabled;
- crash/telemetry product identity if retained.

Required invariant:

An installed production ZCode and a ZAICODE development or packaged build must be able to run at the same time without sharing mutable application state or single-instance ownership.

Do not automatically import or migrate the user's production ZCode profile.

Any future ZCode import must be an explicit operator action.

Keep internal upstream protocol/package names unchanged when renaming them provides no functional benefit.

UPSTREAM UPDATEABILITY IS REQUIRED.

MILESTONE C — ACCOUNT INDEPENDENCE

ZAICODE itself must have no mandatory application account.

Without any Z.ai login, the operator must still be able to:

- start ZAICODE;
- open or create a workspace;
- inspect projects;
- open Settings;
- create and configure agents;
- inspect and edit the ZAICODE work queue;
- configure future SAIFREN integration;
- configure future SAIRoute integration;
- configure future 9router integration;
- configure local/custom providers;
- use provider-backed execution when an independent provider route is configured.

Provider authentication belongs to each provider integration.

ZAICODE application identity must not depend on cloud identity.

PRESERVE Z.AI ONLY AS OPTIONAL PROVIDER

Do not broadly delete Z.ai authentication/provider implementation.

Where practical, preserve Z.ai as one optional external provider.

Its absence must not cause:
- startup failure;
- workspace admission failure;
- disabled agent management;
- disabled queue management;
- forced onboarding;
- forced Connect flow;
- subscription redirects;
- unavailable Settings.

Remove or disable from normal ZAICODE product UX:

- mandatory Connect account CTA;
- Upgrade CTA;
- Coding Plan marketing cards;
- Start Plan marketing;
- subscription price cards;
- account-required onboarding gates;
- Z.ai product-level promotional surfaces;
- assumptions that Z.ai identity owns the application session.

Do not merely hide buttons while backend admission still requires authentication.

Verify the complete startup and workspace path without Z.ai credentials.

MILESTONE D — ZERO-PROVIDER STATE

A fresh ZAICODE profile with zero configured execution providers must be valid.

It should enter the application normally.

Expose a truthful state equivalent to:

"No execution routes configured."

The operator must still be able to configure the application.

Do not redirect into Z.ai registration.

Do not fabricate an active model/provider.

MILESTONE E — ROOT UI.md

Create root:

UI.md

This is mandatory.

It supplements upstream DESIGN.md.

DESIGN.md remains authoritative for visual primitives and design-system behavior.

UI.md is authoritative for ZAICODE product information architecture and operator workflows.

Include at minimum:

# ZAICODE UI

## Product Purpose

ZAICODE is a desktop-first operator workspace for running and supervising coding agents through one graphical interface.

## Account Model

ZAICODE has no mandatory application account.

Cloud authentication is provider-specific.

Workspace access, agent management, queue management, routing configuration, local-provider configuration, and application settings remain available without a Z.ai account.

Z.ai is an optional external provider and has no privileged product-level status inside ZAICODE.

## Execution Architecture

Document the intended conceptual path:

ZAICODE UI
→ Agent / Work Queue
→ SAIFREN
→ SAIRoute
→ 9router
→ Provider / Model
→ Runtime
→ Result

Clearly mark unimplemented layers as planned integration boundaries rather than pretending they already exist.

## Main Workspace

Define:
- Project/Workspace navigation;
- Agent Roster;
- Work Queue;
- Active Runs;
- Inspector;
- Result/Event surface.

## Agent Roster

Visible fields:
- name;
- role;
- state;
- resolved provider;
- resolved model;
- current job;
- queued work count;
- last activity;
- blocker/error state.

## Work Queue

Required conceptual states:
- Draft;
- Queued;
- Ready;
- Running;
- Waiting;
- Blocked;
- Completed;
- Failed;
- Cancelled.

Only expose states that can be backed by actual runtime semantics.

## Routing Visibility

The UI must distinguish:
- configured route;
- selected route;
- resolved provider;
- resolved model;
- fallback selection.

Never display configured intent as actual execution identity when fallback changed the route.

## Main Settings Information Architecture

Target:
- General;
- Appearance;
- Models & Routing;
- Agents;
- SAIFREN;
- SAIRoute;
- 9router;
- Browser;
- Plugins;
- MCP Servers;
- Skills;
- Commands;
- Hooks;
- Usage.

These names describe the intended product IA.

Do not create duplicate pages merely to satisfy labels.

## Modal Policy

Prefer integrated panes, tabs, inline editors, searchable lists, segmented controls, and direct selections.

Avoid unnecessary chains of modal dialogs and combobox-heavy configuration.

## Persistence Ownership

Every UI state must be classified as:
- runtime-authoritative;
- persisted product configuration;
- UI preference;
- derived presentation state.

React components must not become authoritative queue/session storage.

## Startup Behavior

Fresh ZAICODE installation:
1. starts without cloud login;
2. opens the product shell;
3. allows workspace access;
4. allows configuration;
5. truthfully reports missing execution routes.

## Non-Goals

Do not implement:
- distributed schedulers;
- recursive autonomous swarms;
- arbitrary DAG editor;
- new provider stack;
- replacement terminal;
- unrelated upstream redesign;
- mandatory cloud identity.

MILESTONE F — ARCHITECTURE DISCOVERY

Complete docs/ZAICODE_ARCHITECTURE.md before implementing the orchestration layer.

Trace concrete source ownership for:

- Desktop lifecycle;
- Electron identity;
- userData/database roots;
- workspace identity;
- task/session persistence;
- provider registry;
- model selection;
- account provider;
- OAuth;
- custom providers;
- runtime process;
- ZCode agent process;
- subagents;
- task registry;
- background execution;
- completion notification;
- command inbox;
- pending-input queue;
- cancellation;
- retry;
- tool permissions;
- runtime-to-UI transport;
- RPC boundaries;
- UI stores;
- database ownership.

QUEUE CLASSIFICATION

Search all queue-like structures.

For each relevant queue, classify it explicitly as:

- prompt/input queue;
- active execution registry;
- subagent task registry;
- background-task queue;
- notification queue;
- UI presentation queue;
- persistent work queue;
- transient ordering primitive;
- unrelated queue.

Do not assume an existing queue is suitable for ZAICODE work scheduling.

Document whether ZCode currently has a durable cross-agent operator job queue.

If not, state that clearly.

MILESTONE G — ZAICODE AGENT MODEL

Create the smallest persistent ZAICODE agent-definition layer compatible with existing ZCode subagent/runtime architecture.

Required fields should include only what the runtime can use meaningfully:

- stable ID;
- display name;
- role;
- instructions;
- enabled state;
- provider/model route reference;
- reasoning configuration when supported;
- capability/tool policy;
- timestamps if operationally useful.

Do not copy API keys or provider secrets into agent definitions.

Reference provider identities.

Use schema validation.

Persist through a service/repository boundary.

React UI must not write configuration files directly.

Initial templates:

- Coordinator;
- Implementer;
- Auditor;
- Researcher.

Templates are defaults, not privileged hard-coded execution classes.

The operator must be able to create custom agents later.

MILESTONE H — ROUTING ABSTRACTION

Introduce a narrow ZAICODE routing abstraction that can later bind to SAIFREN / SAIRoute / 9router.

Do not invent their final protocols.

The abstraction must be able to represent:

- requested agent;
- requested capability/role;
- configured route;
- resolved provider;
- resolved model;
- fallback reason;
- execution identity;
- failure classification.

The runtime must be able to receive one final resolved provider/model selection through existing provider architecture.

Avoid modifying model-provider internals more than necessary.

Prefer adapters at existing service boundaries.

MILESTONE I — FIRST ZAICODE WORK QUEUE

Implement one authoritative operator-facing queue.

Reuse an existing durable runtime primitive only if architecture discovery proves it satisfies the required semantics.

Otherwise create the smallest durable ZAICODE work-queue service.

Minimum job identity:

- job ID;
- workspace ID;
- assigned agent ID;
- title;
- instructions;
- lifecycle state;
- deterministic order/priority;
- created timestamp;
- started timestamp;
- finished timestamp;
- session/run identity;
- result summary;
- failure/blocker reason;
- retry lineage.

Critical invariants:

- one authoritative owner;
- no accidental duplicate dispatch;
- terminal completion idempotent;
- cancellation idempotent;
- stale completion cannot overwrite a newer retry;
- workspace identity preserved;
- restart cannot silently reinterpret uncertain Running state as success;
- UI presentation is never authoritative execution state;
- notification failure cannot erase actual terminal state.

MILESTONE J — FIRST ZAICODE COCKPIT

Build the first usable integrated UI inside the existing ZCode shared UI architecture.

Do not build another standalone frontend.

Primary surface:

LEFT / COMPACT SIDE
- projects/workspaces;
- agent roster.

CENTER
- queue;
- active work.

RIGHT / COLLAPSIBLE
- selected agent/job inspector.

Expose:
- agent name;
- role;
- state;
- configured route;
- resolved provider/model;
- current job;
- queue depth;
- error/blocker;
- task creation;
- assignment;
- safe reordering;
- cancellation;
- retry;
- result summary.

Do not implement fake Pause.

Only expose Pause if the underlying runtime supports a real resumable pause semantic.

MILESTONE K — SAIFREN / SAIROUTE / 9ROUTER INTEGRATION PREPARATION

Do not duplicate those projects inside ZAICODE.

ZAICODE is their graphical operator shell and orchestration consumer.

Prepare explicit extension points for:

SAIFREN
- agent/pool selection;
- capability selection;
- fallback-agent selection.

SAIRoute
- route policy;
- cost/quality/availability policy;
- provider/model decision.

9router
- provider endpoint execution;
- model inventory;
- provider health;
- concrete routed request.

The first real integration target after contracts are available should be one vertical slice:

ZAICODE UI
→ one ZAICODE agent
→ SAIFREN
→ SAIRoute
→ 9router
→ one provider/model
→ runtime execution
→ streamed result
→ terminal queue state visible in ZAICODE.

Do not start with multi-agent swarm behavior.

One real round trip is the acceptance target for routing integration.

MILESTONE L — STANDALONE BUILD

Produce or verify a standalone ZAICODE development/package path separate from ZCode.

Do not overwrite installed ZCode.

Validate:
- ZAICODE display identity;
- independent userData;
- independent settings;
- independent database;
- independent auth/cookie storage;
- independent logs;
- independent single-instance behavior.

Run ZCode and ZAICODE simultaneously.

Verify no mutable-state collision.

MILESTONE M — RECOVERY TESTS

Test at minimum:

- fresh profile;
- no Z.ai account;
- zero configured providers;
- workspace open;
- Settings open;
- application restart;
- queued jobs across restart;
- uncertain Running job after crash;
- disabled agent with queued jobs;
- missing provider;
- model unavailable;
- cancellation;
- retry;
- duplicate dispatch;
- stale completion after retry;
- malformed persisted agent;
- malformed persisted job;
- workspace switch;
- ZCode and ZAICODE concurrent execution.

Never convert unknown state into success.

MILESTONE N — VALIDATION

Run applicable:

- focused unit tests;
- integration/service tests;
- UI tests;
- E2E tests for changed flows;
- typecheck;
- lint;
- architecture check;
- package/build checks;
- Desktop smoke test.

Preserve baseline-vs-ZAICODE distinction.

Do not claim skipped tests passed.

Do not convert upstream warnings into ZAICODE defects unless the change worsened them.

UPSTREAM DELTA RULE

Keep a ledger of upstream-owned files modified by ZAICODE.

For each:
- file;
- reason;
- why additive extension was insufficient;
- expected rebase risk.

Prefer new ZAICODE modules and centralized capability switches.

Avoid widespread renaming.

Do not mechanically replace "ZCode" in protocol identifiers, internal package names, serialized formats, environment variables, or RPC contracts.

PRODUCT ACCEPTANCE

T-1 is not DONE until at least:

1. upstream baseline receipt is complete;
2. root UI.md exists;
3. docs/ZAICODE_ARCHITECTURE.md exists;
4. ZAICODE has an independent desktop identity;
5. ZAICODE starts without a Z.ai account;
6. workspace access works without login;
7. normal ZAICODE UI contains no mandatory Connect/Upgrade/Coding Plan product gate;
8. Z.ai is optional or safely disabled as a provider;
9. fresh zero-provider state is valid;
10. persistent ZAICODE agent definitions exist;
11. initial agent templates exist;
12. one authoritative operator work queue exists;
13. first cockpit UI can display agents and queue;
14. routing abstraction exists for future SAIFREN/SAIRoute/9router binding;
15. ZCode and ZAICODE can run simultaneously without mutable-state collision;
16. focused validation passes or remaining failures are explicitly classified;
17. current limitations and next exact integration target are recorded.

NEXT EXACT ACTION

Continue T-1 from BUILD.

First complete the baseline receipt and architecture discovery required to make product/account separation safely.

Then implement centralized ZAICODE product identity and account-independent startup before building agent/queue UI.

Do not spend time polishing upstream ZCode commercial screens that will be removed from the ZAICODE primary experience.

